{"tool_name":"chippeakanno","contents":["bioconda","oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"bioconda":{"name":"bioconductor-chippeakanno","version":"3.44.0","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/html/ChIPpeakAnno.html","license":"GPL (>= 2)","summary":"Batch annotation of the peaks identified from either ChIP-seq, ChIP-chip experiments, or any experiments that result in large number of genomic interval data","identifiers":["biotools:chippeakanno"],"description":"The package encompasses a range of functions for identifying the closest gene, exon, miRNA, or custom features\u2014such as highly conserved elements and user-supplied transcription factor binding sites. Additionally, users can retrieve sequences around the peaks and obtain enriched Gene Ontology (GO) or Pathway terms. In version 2.0.5 and beyond, new functionalities have been introduced. These include features for identifying peaks associated with bi-directional promoters along with summary statistics (peaksNearBDP), summarizing motif occurrences in peaks (summarizePatternInPeaks), and associating additional identifiers with annotated peaks or enrichedGO (addGeneIDs). The package integrates with various other packages such as biomaRt, IRanges, Biostrings, BSgenome, GO.db, multtest, and stat to enhance its analytical capabilities.","license_file":"/lib/R/share/licenses/GPL-3","build":{"noarch":"generic","number":0,"rpaths":["lib/R/lib/","lib/"],"run_exports":""},"requirements":{"host":["bioconductor-annotationdbi >=1.72.0,<1.73.0","bioconductor-biocgenerics >=0.56.0,<0.57.0","bioconductor-biomart >=2.66.0,<2.67.0","bioconductor-biostrings >=2.78.0,<2.79.0","bioconductor-ensembldb >=2.34.0,<2.35.0","bioconductor-genomeinfodb >=1.46.0,<1.47.0","bioconductor-genomicalignments >=1.46.0,<1.47.0","bioconductor-genomicfeatures >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-genomicranges >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-graph >=1.88.0,<1.89.0","bioconductor-interactionset >=1.38.0,<1.39.0","bioconductor-iranges >=2.44.0,<2.45.0","bioconductor-keggrest >=1.50.0,<1.51.0","bioconductor-multtest >=2.66.0,<2.67.0","bioconductor-pwalign >=1.6.0,<1.7.0","bioconductor-rbgl >=1.86.0,<1.87.0","bioconductor-regioner >=1.42.0,<1.43.0","bioconductor-rsamtools >=2.26.0,<2.27.0","bioconductor-rtracklayer >=1.70.0,<1.71.0","bioconductor-s4vectors >=0.48.0,<0.49.0","bioconductor-summarizedexperiment >=1.40.0,<1.41.0","bioconductor-universalmotif >=1.28.0,<1.29.0","r-base","r-data.table","r-dbi","r-dplyr","r-ggplot2","r-matrixstats","r-scales","r-stringr","r-tibble","r-tidyr","r-venndiagram"],"run":["bioconductor-annotationdbi >=1.72.0,<1.73.0","bioconductor-biocgenerics >=0.56.0,<0.57.0","bioconductor-biomart >=2.66.0,<2.67.0","bioconductor-biostrings >=2.78.0,<2.79.0","bioconductor-ensembldb >=2.34.0,<2.35.0","bioconductor-genomeinfodb >=1.46.0,<1.47.0","bioconductor-genomicalignments >=1.46.0,<1.47.0","bioconductor-genomicfeatures >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-genomicranges >=1.62.0,<1.63.0","bioconductor-graph >=1.88.0,<1.89.0","bioconductor-interactionset >=1.38.0,<1.39.0","bioconductor-iranges >=2.44.0,<2.45.0","bioconductor-keggrest >=1.50.0,<1.51.0","bioconductor-multtest >=2.66.0,<2.67.0","bioconductor-pwalign >=1.6.0,<1.7.0","bioconductor-rbgl >=1.86.0,<1.87.0","bioconductor-regioner >=1.42.0,<1.43.0","bioconductor-rsamtools >=2.26.0,<2.27.0","bioconductor-rtracklayer >=1.70.0,<1.71.0","bioconductor-s4vectors >=0.48.0,<0.49.0","bioconductor-summarizedexperiment >=1.40.0,<1.41.0","bioconductor-universalmotif >=1.28.0,<1.29.0","r-base","r-data.table","r-dbi","r-dplyr","r-ggplot2","r-matrixstats","r-scales","r-stringr","r-tibble","r-tidyr","r-venndiagram"]},"source_url":["https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/src/contrib/ChIPpeakAnno_3.44.0.tar.gz","https://bioconductor.org/packages/3.22/bioc/src/contrib/Archive/ChIPpeakAnno/ChIPpeakAnno_3.44.0.tar.gz","https://bioarchive.galaxyproject.org/ChIPpeakAnno_3.44.0.tar.gz","https://depot.galaxyproject.org/software/bioconductor-chippeakanno/bioconductor-chippeakanno_3.44.0_src_all.tar.gz"],"test":{"commands":["$R -e \"library('ChIPpeakAnno')\""]},"parent_recipe":{"name":"bioconductor-chippeakanno","path":"recipes/bioconductor-chippeakanno","version":"3.14.2"}},"biotools":{"id":"chippeakanno","home":"http://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/ChIPpeakAnno.html","license":"GPL-2.0","summary":"The package includes functions to retrieve the sequences around the peak, obtain enriched GO terms, find the nearest gene, exon, miRNA or custom features such as most conserved elements and other TF binding sites supplied by users. New functions have been added for finding the peaks with bi-directional promoters with summary statistics, for summarizing the occurrence of motifs in peaks and for adding other IDs to annotated peaks or enrichedGO.","addition_date":"2017-01-17T14:58:03Z","last_update_date":"2019-01-10T04:00:57Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Library"],"version":["3.8.7"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:chippeakanno","credit":[{"email":"Jianhong.ou@umassmed.edu","name":"Jianhong Ou","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"email":"julie.zhu@umassmed.edu","name":"Lihua Julie Zhu","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"http://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/ChIPpeakAnno.html"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence coordinates","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2012"},"format":[{"term":"BED","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3003"},{"term":"GFF","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2305"},{"term":"Sequence range format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2078"}]}],"operation":[{"term":"Gene-set enrichment analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_2436"},{"term":"Sequence annotation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0361"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Nucleic acid features","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_1276"},"format":[{"term":"Textual format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2330"}]},{"data":{"term":"Plot","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2884"},"format":[{"term":"Textual format","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2330"}]}]}],"name":"ChIPpeakAnno","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"bioconductor_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1007/978-1-62703-607-8_8","metadata":{"abstract":"Epigenetic regulation and interactions between transcription factors and regulatory genomic regions play crucial roles in controlling transcriptional regulatory networks that drive development, environmental responses, and disease. Chromatin immunoprecipitation (ChIP) followed by high-throughput sequencing (ChIP-seq) and ChIP followed by genomic tiling microarray hybridization (ChIP-chip) are the two of the most widely used technologies for genome-wide identification of DNA protein interactions and histone modification in vivo. Many algorithms and tools have been developed and evaluated that allow identification of transcription factor binding sites from ChIP-seq or ChIP-chip datasets. However, binding site identification is only the first step; the ultimate goal is to discover the regulatory network of the transcription factor (TF). Here, we present a common workflow for downstream analysis of ChIP-chip and ChIP-seq with an emphasis on annotating binding sites and integration with gene expression data to identify direct and indirect targets of the TF. These tools will help with the overall goal of unraveling transcriptional regulatory networks using datasets publicly available in GEO. \u00a9 2013 Springer Science+Business Media New York.","authors":[{"name":"Zhu L.J."}],"citationCount":37,"date":"2013-09-20T00:00:00Z","journal":"Methods in Molecular Biology","title":"Integrative analysis of ChIP-chip and ChIP-seq dataset"},"pmid":"23975789","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"ChIP-on-chip","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3179"},{"term":"C